  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days (thru Tuesday)...\n\nContinue with a warm and dry forecast through Thursday night.\nRidging aloft today shifts to faster southwest flow on Thursday.\nThe enhanced downslope flow will help to boost temperatures well\ninto the 70s, with a few lower 80s not out of the question over SE\nMT. Record highs are possible Thursday for Miles City (Rec 78) and\nBaker (Rec 71 due to short period of record). South-Southwest\nwinds will orient with the western valleys Thursday afternoon, so\nexpect breezy to gust conditions (20-35mph) for the Paradise \nvalley and western foothills, as well as the higher terrain. \n\nModels still advertising the merger of an upper level system\nmoving northeast out of California, and a Canadian shortwave over\nthe area Friday into early Saturday. However, there has been a\ngradual change in how strong this system will be as it combines\nover the area, with precipitation amounts in the lower elevations\neast of the divide trending significantly downward, especially in\nthe deterministic models. NBM is slowly catching up with this\ndownward trend this morning which is a result in its lag feature.\nTo highlite the changes and the uncertainty that has built into\nthe forecast over the past 24 hours, the precipitation spread in\nthe NBM members for Billings range from a low of 0.01 inches to a\nhigh of 1.9 inches, with the 50th percentile sitting at 0.38\ninches. This shows the latest distribution skewing toward the\nlower end of the range in the latest model runs. The probabilities\nfor more than half an inch of precipitation have decreased over\nthe past 24 hours as well with areas west of a Roundup to Hardin\nline showing 40-50 percent from Friday morning through Saturday\nmorning (was 60-80%). Upslope winds are helping to boost the \nwestern foothills to 60-80% with higher values for the mountains.\nBottom line the trends are lower for amounts, but the probability\nof seeing precipitation remains high for Friday into early \nSaturday.\n\nThe track of the controlling systems have also adjusted to have \nless of a west to east movement overall, further reducing \nprecipitation chances over SE Montana, and bringing a period of \nelevated fire weather concerns to this area for Friday afternoon \nand evening. Southerly winds will gust 30 to 40 mph with RH values\ndropping to around 20% and temperatures now trending warmer than \nThursday for areas along and east of a Broadus to Baker line. \nRecord highs are possible once again Friday afternoon for Miles \nCity (Rec 77) and Baker (Rec 69 due to short period of record) as \nthe cold front looks to hold off across SE Montana until the \nevening hours Friday. \n\nSnowfall amounts have remained fairly consistent over the past 24\nhours with 4 to 8 inches over the western mountains\n(Crazy/Beartooth/Absaroka) and 2 to 4 inches for the Bighorn/Pryor\nmountains. Snow levels drop from 10kft late Fri afternoon, to\nunder 4kft by Saturday morning. However with less precipitation \nand precipitation tapering off faster the lower elevations (below \n6000ft) are looking at little to no snow accumulation with this \nsystem now. However that doesn't mean there won't be some flakes \nmixing in down to around 4kft around sunrise Saturday morning as\nprecipitation is ending. \n\nWith the system changes for early in the weekend the extended\nforecast has trended more uncertain as well. Overall a seasonably\ncool pattern with 20 to 40% chances for precipitation from Sunday\nafternoon through Wednesday. Another upper low may drive up into\nthe area from the 4 corners late Sunday into Monday mainly\nimpacting SE Montana, with a Pacific system pushing into the\nregion Tuesday/Wednesday from the West. A lot of tropical activity\ngoing on in the Pacific which can have significant impacts on\nstorm system moving into our area, and expect the models to\nstruggle in the longer ranges as long as that activity continues.\nSo, consider uncertainty to be at a higher level than usual for \nthe forecast beyond Saturday. Chambers",
